# Commons Dashboard → Mastodon Deep Link Routing Spec

## Purpose

The Commons Dashboard is a **guided control surface** that:

- takes the creator to the correct Mastodon admin page (deep link)
- optionally shows suggested copy (pasteable)
- optionally verifies or auto-applies via API later

**Mastodon remains the system of record.**

---

# Key Assumptions

1. The creator may arrive at the dashboard **not logged into Mastodon**.
2. Admin deep links require an authenticated Mastodon session.
3. We will support:
   - **MVP:** “Open Mastodon page + paste + save + Mark done”
   - **Future:** auto-apply + verify state via API

---

# Link Types

We support three link classes:

## A) Public instance links
No auth required.

Examples:
- `https://<instance_domain>/about`
- `https://<instance_domain>/explore`

## B) User UI links (logged-in user)
Require Mastodon login but not necessarily admin.

Examples:
- `https://<instance_domain>/home`
- `https://<instance_domain>/settings/profile`

## C) Admin panel links (admin session required)
Require admin permissions and authenticated session.

Examples:
- `https://<instance_domain>/admin/settings/appearance`
- `https://<instance_domain>/admin/settings/registrations`
- `https://<instance_domain>/admin/custom_emojis`

---

# Canonical Deep Link Object

Every checklist item that opens Mastodon uses this contract:

```json
{
  "id": "about_text",
  "label": "About text",
  "purpose": "This becomes the server description + sets expectations for new members.",
  "link": {
    "type": "admin",
    "path": "/admin/settings/about",
    "requires_auth": true
  },
  "suggested_copy": "…",
  "completion": {
    "mode": "self_reported",
    "status": "suggested"
  }
}

Fields:
  • link.type: "public" | "user" | "admin"
  • link.path: must begin with /
  • requires_auth: true for "user" and "admin"

⸻

URL Construction

Given:
  • instance_domain (from ProvisioningMetadata)
  • path (from link object)

Construct:

target_url = https://{instance_domain}{path}

Rules:
  • Always use https://
  • No query params unless explicitly required for a page
  • Never hardcode domain strings in the front end

⸻

Auth Gate Behavior (MVP)

Dashboard rule

Never assume Mastodon session exists.

When the creator presses Open on an admin link:
  1.  Open the deep link in a new tab:
  • https://<instance_domain><path>
  2.  If Mastodon redirects to login:
  • creator logs in
  • then continues to destination (Mastodon should return them after login)

Dashboard copy for the first item

The first checklist item should always be:

Sign in to Mastodon
  • Purpose: “Admin links only work after you sign in.”

Deep link:
  • https://<instance_domain>/auth/sign_in (or the correct login route for your Mastodon build)

Completion:
  • self-reported (“Mark done”)

This trains the creator and avoids confusing failures.

⸻

Auth Gate Behavior (SSO-Integrated)

Once unified auth is in place (V’s plan):

We still retain the same UX rule:

The dashboard checks if the Mastodon session is valid before opening admin deep links.

Recommended implementation options:

Option 1 (simple): always open, rely on redirect
  • Keep MVP behavior
  • Least coupling
  • Works even if session detection fails

Option 2 (better): preflight check before opening admin links

Dashboard performs a lightweight probe:
  • GET https://<instance_domain>/api/v1/accounts/verify_credentials

If:
  • 200 → session exists → open admin link
  • 401/403 → no session → open /auth/sign_in instead

Notes:
  • this requires CORS handling or proxying via Commons backend
  • recommended to implement via backend proxy for security + simplicity

⸻

“Open” Button Behavior

The “Open” button does exactly one thing:
  • open the deep link (or login route if auth check fails)

It does NOT:
  • mark the item done
  • attempt to apply suggested copy
  • validate the content

That separation keeps MVP clean.

⸻

Completion (“Mark done”) Behavior

MVP: completion is user-declared.

When the creator presses Mark done:
  • set payload status = applied
  • hide suggested copy (optional UX)
  • keep a “Show suggested copy” toggle for review

No API verification in MVP.

⸻

Future: Apply + Verify Modes

A checklist item can specify:

"completion": {
  "mode": "self_reported | verify | auto_apply_then_verify",
  "status": "suggested | applied | skipped"
}

verify
  • dashboard (or backend) checks Mastodon state via API
  • if matches → status becomes applied

auto_apply_then_verify
  • backend applies the setting during provisioning or on-demand
  • then verify and mark applied

Even in these modes, Mastodon remains source of truth.

⸻

Handling Link Drift (Mastodon upgrades)

Admin paths may vary across Mastodon versions.

Rules:
  1.  All path values must live in a single routing map file:
  • mastodon_admin_routes.json or routes.ts
  2.  Checklist items reference route keys, not raw paths:

Example:

"link": {
  "type": "admin",
  "route_key": "ADMIN_SETTINGS_ABOUT"
}

Routing map:

{
  "ADMIN_SETTINGS_ABOUT": "/admin/settings/about"
}

This lets you patch routes centrally if Mastodon changes.

⸻

Error Handling UX

If the opened page returns 404 or permission error

Dashboard should show:

“Couldn’t open that admin page.
Try signing in again, or use Mastodon’s admin menu.”

Plus a button:
  • “Open Mastodon admin home” → /admin

This prevents dead ends.

⸻

Recommended Checklist Deep Links (Starter Set)

Admin links (likely):
  • /admin (admin home)
  • /admin/settings/about
  • /admin/settings/appearance
  • /admin/settings/registrations
  • /admin/settings/discovery (or equivalent)
  • /admin/settings/branding (or equivalent)
  • /admin/custom_emojis (optional)

User profile link (host account):
  • /settings/profile

Public links:
  • /about
  • /@<owner_username> (profile page)
  • /explore

(Exact routes should be confirmed against the Mastodon version you deploy.)

⸻

Summary

The dashboard is a routing and guidance layer.
  • Build URLs from instance_domain + path
  • Always include a “Sign in to Mastodon” step
  • In MVP, rely on redirect-to-login and self-reported completion
  • Over time, add preflight session checks + apply/verify via API
  • Centralize route paths behind route keys to avoid breakage

